WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.468
-Hey, Charlize.
Nice to see you again.

00:00:02.468 --> 00:00:04.470
-Hi. Nice to see you.
How are you?

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:06.039
-I'm well. Obviously,

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:08.341
I would prefer to be
seeing you in person.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.476
I do appreciate
you making the time.

00:00:10.476 --> 00:00:13.046
Are you having
an enjoyable summer?

00:00:13.046 --> 00:00:15.214
-Yes, I am. Listen, I mean,

00:00:15.214 --> 00:00:18.017
not without mentioning

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:21.053
everything that we've
been going through,

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:23.289
I will say it's been
really, really nice

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:25.658
to spend time with my kids
and to just be home.

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:27.660
I'm always craving more of that.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.697
So I've taken
full advantage of that.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.064
-And it should be noted

00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:33.666
that when you have
a big movie like this,

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:35.168
someone like you
who's the star of it

00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:37.403
would have to be doing
so much press on the road.

00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:41.173
It must be kind of nice to
just be able to do it from home.

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:44.110
-Yeah. I don't think we're
going to go back to the other.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.444
[ Laughs ]
-Yeah.

00:00:45.444 --> 00:00:47.446
I mean, it makes sense
to have to leave home

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:50.015
for six months to make a movie,
but it always seems like

00:00:50.015 --> 00:00:51.851
such a bummer
to have to then leave home

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:53.686
for another two months
to talk about it.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.254
-Yeah, I don't know.
I might disagree with you.

00:00:55.254 --> 00:00:56.656
I'm trying to figure out
how I can just do

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.158
everything from here.
-Oh, even the movie?

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:00.793
Well, I should stress

00:01:00.793 --> 00:01:03.229
that I feel like
I really enjoyed this movie.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.197
I loved the comic
it was based on.

00:01:05.197 --> 00:01:07.934
I feel like "The Old Guard,"
if you shot it at home,

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:10.269
would be considerably worse.

00:01:10.269 --> 00:01:12.471
-[ Laughs ]
Now, that's a good friend.

00:01:12.471 --> 00:01:14.340
You just gave me
the honest God truth,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.742
so I will take that.

00:01:16.742 --> 00:01:22.215
-You, uh, play one of
a group of immortal soldiers.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:23.582
-Yeah.

00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:27.187
-Your character may be,
um, 6,000-plus years old.

00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:30.022
Ya look great.
I don't know what the secret is.

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:33.926
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:37.263
-Do you ever -- Like,
playing a character like this,

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:39.832
did you think about what
it would actually be like?

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:41.334
Would you be somebody

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:45.071
who would be in for another
6,000 years of living?

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:47.573
-Mnh. I don't know about that.

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:51.244
I think, you know,
when you just think about it,

00:01:51.244 --> 00:01:54.080
my reaction would be,
"Oh, my gosh!

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.982
More time
with people that I love."

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:58.784
Like, of course,
that sounds really nice.

00:01:58.784 --> 00:02:05.692
But, ultimately, I think having
to live for a very long time

00:02:05.692 --> 00:02:08.094
must feel incredibly lonely.

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:11.530
And I think the idea
of losing people that you love

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:13.866
over and over and over again,

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:16.402
I would have a really
hard time with that.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:19.138
-Yeah, and the movie,
it should be noted --

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:22.108
This is a super-fun
action movie.

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:24.644
But it's also really sad
at times,

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.213
and I think it does a nice job
of looking at that, as well.

00:02:27.213 --> 00:02:29.849
I also want to give you
a shout-out for this --

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:31.618
As an action-movie star,

00:02:31.618 --> 00:02:34.554
which, let's be honest,
you fully are now.

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:37.256
I feel like
you are at the forefront

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:40.360
of fight scenes that take place
in enclosed places.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.262
I feel like you've had some

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:46.165
really good stairway fights
in some films.

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:49.068
This has an airplane fight,
a hallway fight.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:51.437
You're getting very good
at close fighting.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.005
-Wow. Thank you.

00:02:53.005 --> 00:02:56.275
You're just coming
with so many compliments.

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:58.244
Can we just FaceTime
every morning?

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.079
Can I start my day with you?
-Yeah.

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.281
I would super be down for it.
Yeah.

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:03.983
-It is fun. It is actually --

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:07.086
The challenge when you have
a set piece kind of dictate

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:09.488
the space that you're going to
be able to utilize,

00:03:09.488 --> 00:03:12.325
it's really fun to have that.

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:13.626
Um, yeah.

00:03:13.626 --> 00:03:15.561
I mean, listen,
I am inspired by fights.

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:18.197
Like, you know the bathroom
fight in "Mission"?

00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:19.365
-Yeah.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.434
-Yeah. I mean,
I love fights like that.

00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:24.370
So, um, I think it's always --

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:26.371
The set piece plays
a huge part in it.

00:03:26.371 --> 00:03:28.240
And when you have
a lot of space,

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:30.510
I think you lose
some of that intensity

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.945
of, like, people being
right on top of each other

00:03:32.945 --> 00:03:35.682
in the hand-to-hand
combat style.

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:38.717
So, where else can we
have a fight scene?

00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:40.219
Hmm.
-That's true.

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:42.455
I feel like --
I mean, you guys --

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:44.690
'Cause I feel like there's
obviously the bathroom fight

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.692
scene in "Mission."

00:03:46.692 --> 00:03:48.728
And then this has
an airplane fight scene.

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:51.864
But have we seen a really good
airplane-bathroom fight scene?

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.465
-[ Laughs ]
-That would be like --

00:03:53.465 --> 00:03:57.369
Now we're making it as small --
-Now we are literally just --

00:03:57.369 --> 00:03:59.439
It's body-to-body contact.

00:03:59.439 --> 00:04:02.575
-Yeah, exactly.
-That...amazing, though.

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:06.012
-No one wins in
an airplane-bathroom fight.

00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:08.348
That is one of the adages.
-Yeah.

00:04:08.348 --> 00:04:10.516
No, everyone's a loser
in that for sure.

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:12.284
-You have an ax in this film.

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:15.121
And, uh, you seem
very comfortable with the ax.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:19.158
Does something --
Does work go into looking

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.227
like you know what
you're doing with an ax?

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:22.961
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:22.961 --> 00:04:28.467
It's a mixture of work,
and then also I was advised

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:32.404
by my incredible fight
coordinator, Danny Hernandez,

00:04:32.404 --> 00:04:36.175
to just walk around with the ax
at all times.

00:04:36.175 --> 00:04:37.743
So he gave me an ax.

00:04:37.743 --> 00:04:41.314
It was rubber, but it still
looked very, very real.

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:44.149
And he just said, "Listen, just
keep it with you at all times."

00:04:44.149 --> 00:04:46.552
Like, he's like,
"Even if you go for a drive,

00:04:46.552 --> 00:04:49.655
if you go to a restaurant" --
This was back before lockdown.

00:04:49.655 --> 00:04:51.990
"Like, if you go gro--
Just have it in the car

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.260
even if you just hold it, or if
you're in the kitchen cooking,

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.028
like, just twirl it
a little bit."

00:04:56.028 --> 00:04:59.498
So, you know, I take direction
very seriously.

00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:01.700
So I did that.

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.970
And then I remember one night
I went out to dinner,

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:06.472
and the valet
pulled my car around,

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:10.242
and he just got out of the car,
and he just -- his face just --

00:05:10.242 --> 00:05:13.379
He didn't say anything, and
I couldn't quite understand it.

00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:14.780
I was like, "Did I tip him?

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.183
Is it because I --
Did I not tip enough?"

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:19.518
And then I got in the car, and
the big ax was just lying there.

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:21.453
And I was like, "Ohh!"
-Yeah.

00:05:21.453 --> 00:05:24.890
And it should be noted
it's not the kind of ax

00:05:24.890 --> 00:05:27.793
where somebody might've thought
you were doing some woodworking.

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:30.963
It's definitely, like --
It's definitely a menace ax.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:36.169
-Yeah.
It's like a prehistoric, like...

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:37.670
-Yeah.
-...double-faced ax

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:40.406
that just looks like it is going
to hurt you by looking at it.

